md5sum filename.doc
sha256sum filename.doc
file filename.doc

  
exiftool filename.doc
exiftool: Extracts metadata from files. It can reveal the origin or embedded details

oleid filename.doc
oleid Flags potentially harmful characteristics: 
                   a) File format type 
                   b) Presence of macros (VBA Macros) 
                   c) External relationships (files embedded in other file types)

olevba filename.doc
olevba filename.doc > newfile.vba
olevba --deobf --reveal newfile.vba > new_deobf.vba
olevba analyzing the VBA code for potential security issues in malicious Office documents and displaes any findings in the output


olemeta filename.doc
Olemeta etrieves file metadata to detect macros or suspicious embeddings
  
strings filename.doc, (strings -n 5 baddoc.doc)
strings: Searches for suspicious patterns, such as: 
                    a) Macro names (VB_Name) ------ | grep vbs
                    b) IPs, URLs, User Agents ----- | grep https, http
                    c) Keywords like AutoOpen (triggers macros)
                    d) ping IP commands


xorsearch filename.doc http
xorsearch: Detects encoded or obfuscated content (e.g., hidden HTTP strings or embedded executables)



Open file location in terminal
Identify and Hash the File: Calculate the MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 hash of the document
MIME-type dont trust file type, check it 
Check against threat intelligence databases to see if it is already identified as malicious.
Inspect File Type and Metadata: Use tools like file or hex editors to verify the file extension matches the actual content (e.g., a .doc that is actually an .exe). 
Check metadata for suspicious creation dates or author names.
Extract Strings: Use strings utilities to scan for readable text, including IP addresses, URLs, domain names, or command-line arguments (e.g., powershell.exe, cmd.exe) that indicate downloader functionality.
Analyze Macros and Scripts: For MS Office docs, use tools like oletools (e.g., olevba) to extract VBA macros. Look for AutoOpen, Document_Open, or suspicious shell commands.
De-obfuscate Code: Analyze embedded scripts (VBScript, PowerShell, JavaScript) that are often obfuscated. Use static analysis tools to decode or de-obfuscate to reveal the final payload.
Identify Suspicious Structure: Use tools to detect if the document uses OLE (Object Linking and Embedding) to load external malicious code or exploits, often indicated by abnormally structured containers. 
